# code-walkthrough.nvim

Code walkthroughs for Neovim 0.11+ with Claude Code and Codex. A Markdown side panel shows bounded explanation pages while the source window highlights the selected code range. No Lua dependencies. Uses your installed and authenticated `claude` or `codex` CLI.

Run `python3 install/install.py` to install into your Neovim configuration and add the skill to Claude Code and Codex discovery directories. Existing files are backed up when replaced. Restart Neovim after installation. The default provider is Claude; change the installed plugin spec's `opts.provider` to `"codex"` for a persistent default.

| Key | Action |
| --- | --- |
| `<leader>ah` | Toggle explanations / load `.nvim/walkthrough.json` |
| `<leader>aj`, `<leader>ak` | Next / previous explanation box |
| `<leader>an`, `<leader>ap` | Next / previous snippet |
| `<leader>ar` | Ask about the highlighted range |
| `<leader>a]`, `<leader>a[` | Next / previous explanation page |
| `]p`, `[p`, Ctrl-F, Ctrl-B | Turn pages when focused in the panel |
| `q` | Hide the focused panel |

`:WalkthroughGenerate explain my changes` generates a walkthrough from the current buffer and `git diff HEAD` (staged and unstaged tracked changes). The buffer should be saved first: ranges are validated against loaded files. New files are included when they are the current buffer. For a complete multi-file walkthrough, ask either agent to use its `code-walkthrough` skill; it reads the relevant files and writes the watched JSON file. For example: “Use code-walkthrough to walk me through the changes in Neovim.”

`:WalkthroughProvider codex` and `:WalkthroughProvider claude` switch providers for new requests. `:WalkthroughAsk <question>` also accepts a question directly. `:WalkthroughCancel` cancels a pending request. Requests time out after three minutes by default. Provider errors are shown through Neovim notifications. CLI authentication and network access are required for live responses.

`:WalkthroughLoad [path]` loads a walkthrough. The expected location is `<project>/.nvim/walkthrough.json`; custom paths must also be in a directory one level below the project root. The file format and authoring instructions are in [the skill](skills/code-walkthrough/SKILL.md). The watcher opens a newly authored walkthrough and refreshes it when replaced. Overview boxes highlight the entire snippet; subsection boxes highlight their own range. Navigation stops at the first and last entry.

Questions include the current range, overview, current explanation and previous snippet questions. Answers become additional boxes on that snippet and use the same pagination. Answers live in memory until the walkthrough is reloaded or Neovim exits. Editing source displays a warning to regenerate because saved ranges may become stale. Toggling explanations off clears highlights and leaves source buffers open.

Claude requests run with tools disabled. Codex requests use its read-only sandbox and ephemeral JSON event output, following the [non-interactive CLI interface](https://developers.openai.com/codex/noninteractive). The plugin sends prompts via stdin without shell interpolation. It never bypasses CLI permissions. Code and diff context are sent to the selected provider when you generate or ask a question.

Manual setup with any plugin manager: add this directory to `runtimepath`, then call `require("code-walkthrough").setup({ provider = "claude", width = 0.42, page_lines = 18, timeout = 180000, poll_ms = 1500 })`.

Run checks from the repository root:

```sh
nvim --clean --headless -l tools/code-walkthrough.nvim/tests/run.lua
python3 tools/code-walkthrough.nvim/install/install.py --home /tmp/walkthrough-install-test
```

Tests use provider fixtures and fake local processes; they make no paid model calls.
